ionUUID->"46e6a64c-bc5b-4de7-978e-013a6de99b2f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "On ",
 StyleBox["Z^d", "TI"],
 " the count is ",
 StyleBox["L_d(r) = \[CapitalSigma]_k 2^k C(d, k) C(r, k)", "TI"],
 ", the sum over ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " from 0 to ",
 StyleBox["d", "TI"],
 ", with ",
 StyleBox["C", "TI"],
 " the binomial coefficient: a point of the ball has ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " nonzero coordinates, which take ",
 StyleBox["C(d, k)", "TI"],
 " positions, ",
 StyleBox["2^k", "TI"],
 " signs and ",
 StyleBox["C(r, k)", "TI"],
 " absolute values of sum at most ",
 StyleBox["r", "TI"],
 ". The substrates are finite patches of the lattices. A count follows the \
polynomial while the ball stays inside the patch, and the Riemannian count \
while one more layer does: on the medium cubic grid, to ",
 StyleBox["r = 3", "TI"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["r = 2", "TI"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"5135f5ba-df82-4db5-a3ac-5a7a769a5895"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In the continuum the reference is the small-ball expansion of Gray and \
Vanhecke. On a Riemannian manifold of dimension ",
 StyleBox["n", "TI"],
 ", with ",
 StyleBox["\[Omega]_n", "TI"],
 " the volume of the Euclidean unit ball and ",
 StyleBox["Scal(c)", "TI"],
 " the scalar curvature at the centre, ",
 StyleBox["Vol B_r(c) = \[Omega]_n r^n (1 \[Minus] Scal(c) r\.b2 / (6(n + 2)) \
+ O(r\:2074))", "TI"],
 ". A lattice ball is not a discrete round ball: the scaled path metric of a \
lattice converges to a norm whose unit ball is a square on the square grid \
and a hexagon on the triangular one, and the leading coefficient of ",
 StyleBox["L", "TI"],
 " is the area of that unit ball. The expansion is the reference on a mesh of \
a curved surface."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"e96e5e2c-100c-46ec-85a7-65440d67ea9c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "How the number is measured: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"InfraMeasurement", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"InfraBall", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["c", "TI"], ",", " ", 
      StyleBox["r", "TI"]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["measure", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "8c1ac3ed-9f83-44d2-aff7-736bd95dc392"],
 " takes the vertices at distance at most ",
 StyleBox["r", "TI"],
 " from ",
 StyleBox["c", "TI"],
 ", one row of the distance matrix cut at ",
 StyleBox["r", "TI"],
 ", and counts them, or counts its ",
 Cell[BoxData["InfraInterior"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "27b543c1-4ac1-4d2a-92f3-88a74ca8313b"],
 ". The profile is the list over ",
 StyleBox["r = 0, 1, \[Ellipsis]", "TI"],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData["LogDifferenceQuotients"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "287c3043-e1a3-46f1-98e1-a029dc312551"],
 " reads the dimension off it. On a lattice the quotients of the counting \
profile from ",
 StyleBox["r = 0", "TI"],
 " and of the Riemannian profile from ",
 StyleBox["r = 1", "TI"],
 " coincide: they are the curve of the Wolfram Physics technical \
introduction, section 4.5, which approaches the dimension from above."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"7fd84880-5246-4112-ab89-19c11043978f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A radius past the eccentricity gives the whole graph, and then both \
measures are the number of vertices: the rim of the graph is not a boundary \
of the ball. A band with ",
 StyleBox["r > s", "TI"],
 " gives the empty set."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"231faf4a-03d2-4803-9a08-23b98b49b423"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The head holds the centre and the radius and computes nothing. A ball has \
one member, the vertex set, so ",
 Cell[BoxData["FindInfraRepresentative"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "29c49b41-e1e2-446e-8277-82ef2df7b1ad"],
 " gives it as a sorted vertex list and ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Faithful\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "0cc4be76-3c5a-4812-98db-b1a9b76d8f06"],
 " is ",
 Cell[BoxData["True"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "856d9cd4-2d40-413e-a9e3-a8e8b7c8c8af"],
 ". ",
 Cell[BoxData["InfraMeasurement"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ccc16cde-2ebf-4ceb-b643-4c88529089fe"],
 " also reads ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<VertexDensity\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "aa53988e-19e6-4369-a050-65aa0a5d8dcc"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<EdgeDensity\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1b64cd84-11fd-40a8-bce2-501eb46e5578"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Cardinality\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "9649beb6-cec6-464f-8e3e-9c519e60e184"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Subgraph\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "a371b8c9-4894-4552-ac39-1821f68d651b"],
 "; a ball has no ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Graph\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "80618aa2-241d-46aa-bc34-5d863b84f20d"],
 " and no ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Length\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "31920847-360c-4a4c-933f-098c895ca640"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"d26c5e1d-833e-48b2-a206-199adf418845"],

Cell["Corresponding notions in the classical axiom systems:", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"1526f6d2-f04b-4328-81f7-e216a391c30d"],

